window.docContents[248].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity":{"contents":"\n\nElliptic regularity reconnects the variational and classical theories: a weak\nsolution produced by Lax–Milgram gains as many Sobolev derivatives as the\ncoefficients and data permit.\n\nDifference quotients are defined on  functions, even when pointwise\nderivatives are unavailable, and their product identities separate the\nsolution, coefficients, and cutoffs. They regularize within the energy space,\nwith no mollification. Cutoff functions introduce commutator terms, boundary\ncharts introduce Jacobian factors, and\nlower-order coefficients introduce additional forcing.\n\nEvans (2010, Sections 6.3.1–6.3.2, pp. 327–343) proves interior \nregularity for arbitrary  weak solutions, global boundary regularity\nfor homogeneous Dirichlet solutions, higher-order Sobolev regularity, and\nsmoothness under smooth data. We prove Evans's compact-set interior theorem\nfor arbitrary  weak solutions, and also a zero-trace version localized\nby an atlas cutoff, which is the form the global Dirichlet assembly uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators","header":"5.6.1. Interior and Boundary Regularity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity"},"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Constant-coefficient-differential-operators":{"contents":"Let  be a finite index set. For each , choose a coefficient\n, an order , and directions\n. The associated constant-coefficient\noperator isWhenever the ambient topological module carries continuous line derivatives,\n is a continuous linear endomorphism.\n\nFor every admissible ,\n\nMathlib provides each iterated directional derivative as\nLineDeriv.iteratedLineDerivOpCLM. Fixed scalar multiplication and finite\nsums of continuous linear maps assemble these terms into . Evaluation\nof the finite sum gives the displayed action formula.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.5. Constant-coefficient differential operators","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Constant-coefficient-differential-operators"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Formal-Jets-and-Residual-Losses":{"contents":"\n\nA residual loss is an ordinary real-valued function, differentiated with\nthe analytic Fréchet derivative. A formal jet instead stores coefficients;\nidentifying them with derivatives requires a separate semantic theorem.\n\nResidual losses are ordinary functions.\n\nA pointwise residual energy is the square of a LinearPDE's totalized residual\nexpression. A sampled loss sums these energies over a finite list of points.\nInterpreting this objective as a classical PDE loss requires a separate\nregularity proof for the candidate field. Given a parameterized model\n, its objective is the ordinary composition\n.\n\nA zero sampled loss can still miss the equation between sample points. For\nthe one-dimensional equation  on , choose an integer\n and , and set\n\n\n\nAt every grid point , the residual vanishes, so the sampled\nsquared loss is zero. Between these points its magnitude reaches ,\nwhich can be arbitrarily large. The candidate is smooth and even has zero\nvalues at both endpoints. A uniform residual enclosure would detect this\noscillation; sampling alone cannot. Converting such a uniform enclosure to\nsolution accuracy then needs a stability estimate in the relevant norm.\n\nFor a differentiable objective  on a real Hilbert space,\nthe gradient-descent map isIn Euclidean coordinates, the Adam-style map first sets  and computesBoth are total update functions defined from the analytic gradient. An\nanalytic use of the Adam-style map must separately prove the sign and nonvanishing facts it\nneeds for .\n\nIn applications, sampledLoss builds a real-valued objective from an\nalready defined PDE residual and a finite sample. A gradient step then has\nthe form\n\n\n\nwith the analytic gradient, so a residual-loss theorem rests on no\nautomatic-differentiation compiler.\n\nHigher-order multivariate jets.\n\nThe jet layer uses Mathlib's multivariate formal power series, so variables\nmay be indexed by an arbitrary type  rather than only by Fin n.\n\nFor a multi-index , an order- jet is a\nformal coefficient family . When a separate semantic theorem\nidentifies these coefficients with derivatives, the normalization convention\nisFormal products use ordinary power-series convolution:The order index controls finite extraction and finite formal composition.\nThe underlying representation is a full formal power series: coefficients\nabove the index may be nonzero, and the carrier itself contains no function,\nbase point, differentiability proof, or convergence theorem.\n\nFor a Euclidean scalar function , the proposition\nthat a formal jet  represents  to first order at  recordsThe function, point, and jet are parameters of the proposition. Only the\nvalue and the first coefficients are constrained.\n\nThe expression compiler maps every constructor of the verified real\nexpression language to an arbitrary-order multivariate formal jet. Arithmetic\nnodes use formal power-series algebra. A unary elementary function is lifted\nby finite formal composition with coefficients computed from normalized\nsymbolic derivatives of the corresponding one-variable primitive.Under sufficient differentiability and Taylor-remainder hypotheses, this is the familiar finite\ncomposition formulaCompilation is algebraically total, but analytic claims retain the original\nValidAt conditions. In particular, division requires a nonzero denominator,\nthe real logarithm requires a nonzero argument, and the classical square-root\nrule requires a positive argument.\n\nFor every positive order and Euclidean expression valid at the base point,\nthe compiled jet agrees with the\nfunction value and all first-coordinate derivatives, and the function is\ndifferentiable on a neighborhood. Higher coefficients remain formal\npower-series coefficients unless another theorem identifies their analytic\nmeaning. For example, the exponential jet has fourth coefficient\n\n\n\nThis is a coefficient identity; it does not assert a general higher-order\nTaylor remainder theorem.\n\nAn interval jet assigns a rational interval to each formal multivariate\ncoefficient. Encloses F J means that every coefficient through the declared\norder lies in its interval:The interval operations are explicit rather than typeclass ring operations.\nThis is necessary because interval multiplication is sound but not\ndistributive.\n\nConstants, coordinate seeding, addition, subtraction, negation, and\npower-series convolution preserve coefficient enclosures. At positive order,\nextracting the constant and first coefficients gives interval values and an\noutput-first interval Jacobian for a vector residual. These intervals contain\nthe corresponding exact formal coefficients. For a scalar jet that\nrepresents the function to first order,\nthey also enclose its value and partial derivatives. These guarantees hold\nat a single input point.\n\nThese pointwise enclosures can supply data for validated root finding. The\nKrawczyk certificate proves existence\nand uniqueness of a zero in a closed ball from Jacobian enclosures that hold\nthroughout the ball, together with self-mapping and strict contraction\ninequalities; a jet certificate supplies the corresponding data at one point.\n\nRelationship to computer-assisted PDE proofs.\n\nGómez-Serrano's survey of computer-assisted proofs in PDE describes a pipeline\nthat combines interval arithmetic, high-order automatic differentiation,\nrigorous quadrature, singular-integral decompositions, validated root finding,\nand problem-specific analytic estimates. PDEAtlas has checked foundations for\nseveral of these layers:\n\n* exact rational interval arithmetic and scalar elementary-function\nenclosures;* exact symbolic differentiation with explicit analytic domains;* arbitrary-index, arbitrary-order exact multivariate jets;* polynomial interval-jet algebra and certified residual/Jacobian extraction;* closed-ball Krawczyk certificates with interval-matrix residual bounds;* Taylor models and derivative-bound automation, chiefly in one real variable;* rigorous one-dimensional Simpson and Gauss–Legendre quadrature; and* abstract compact-operator, Fredholm, Hilbert-basis, and spectral theory.\n\nThe survey's Muskat and SQG results further use principal-value\nsingular-integral splitting, multidimensional interval-Newton solvers,\ncomputer-validated spectral gaps, and a quantitative Crandall–Rabinowitz\nbifurcation argument.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.3. Formal Jets and Residual Losses","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Formal-Jets-and-Residual-Losses"}});